      "text": "Given the function:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\n\\]\nwe need to determine its range.\n\nFirst, let's analyze the function step by step.\n\n1. **Simplify the expression inside the arctan function:**\n\n   Consider the term \\(\\arctan x\\). We know that:\n   \\[\n   \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n   \\]\n   for \\(x > 0\\). This identity can be derived from the tangent addition formula:\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\left( \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\tan \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) = 1\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\left( \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\frac{x + \\frac{1}{x}}{1 - x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}} = \\frac{x + \\frac{1}{x}}{1 - 1} = \\frac{x + \\frac{1}{x}}{0}\n   \\]\n   This is undefined when \\(x = 1\\), and the sum simplifies to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) for \\(x > 0\\).\n\n2. **Substitute \\(\\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\) into the arctan function:**\n\n   We need to find:\n   \\[\n   \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\n   \\]\n   Using the identity \\(\\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\), we can see that:\n   \\[\n   \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n   \\]\n   for \\(x > 0\\).\n\n3. **Consider the range of \\(\\arctan\\) functions:**\n\n   The function \\(\\arctan x\\) has a range of \\((- \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\). Therefore, the sum \\(\\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\) will range from \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nThus, the range of the function \\(f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}}\n\\]",
